(6490305, 963897, 108580, '<td valign="top">Well done again. Massive amounts of patience and then the skill to get "the shot" should not be discounted.</td>', 'Well done again. Massive amounts of patience and then the skill to get "the shot" should not be discounted.', '2011-08-03 04:24:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(6490311, 963902, 93565, '<td valign="top">At its heart, this challenge was a Free Study that required your title link the image with a sense. I say this because we sense the world, and all of our photos are necessarily of the world. Anything can be sensed that we are depicting. What this means, though, is that it becomes a Free Study- where grandiose and visually stunning (eye candy) photos dominate. As such, your photo is neither. Despite how harsh that sounds, hear me out. Your scene is not something we''ve never seen before, nor is it superbly eye catching, which is a requisite for a high score in a Free Study. It is a well taken photo, no doubt, and I surely enjoy it as an entry.<br/>While I understand that you intended for the foot to be the focal point, the sharpness of her face in the background demands our attention, and the viewer is left going between the two. A wider aperture would have helped this (shooting at 1.4 or 1.8), though this was not an option given your lens choice. I like the fun feel of this shot, and its interesting perspective. Because of this, I most likely would have given it a 5 or possibly a 6 if it struck the mood I was in when I voted.<br/> <br/>My advice is to view your entries in the full context of the challenge. It is very rare that an audience as varied as DPC will unanimously interpret your image how you''d like them to. There will always be those who see things differently, and this is not bad, it is great. Criticizing the variety of interpretations is silly- you, nor I, nor any one person, are not the arbiter of art, or photography, or all things under the sun. Some images will resonate with some users, some won''t. A failure on your behalf to predict the reaction of the masses does not mean your photo is without merit, and some would say this in fact means it deserves more attention for that reason alone, but don''t take this to mean that it is necessarily the audience that is to blame. The onus is on the artist to provide a framework for the viewer.</td>', 'At its heart, this challenge was a Free Study that required your title link the image with a sense. I say this because we sense the world, and all of our photos are necessarily of the world. Anything can be sensed that we are depicting. What this means, though, is that it becomes a Free Study- where grandiose and visually stunning (eye candy) photos dominate. As such, your photo is neither. Despite how harsh that sounds, hear me out. Your scene is not something we''ve never seen before, nor is it superbly eye catching, which is a requisite for a high score in a Free Study. It is a well taken photo, no doubt, and I surely enjoy it as an entry.While I understand that you intended for the foot to be the focal point, the sharpness of her face in the background demands our attention, and the viewer is left going between the two. A wider aperture would have helped this (shooting at 1.4 or 1.8), though this was not an option given your lens choice. I like the fun feel of this shot, and its interesting perspective. Because of this, I most likely would have given it a 5 or possibly a 6 if it struck the mood I was in when I voted. My advice is to view your entries in the full context of the challenge. It is very rare that an audience as varied as DPC will unanimously interpret your image how you''d like them to. There will always be those who see things differently, and this is not bad, it is great. Criticizing the variety of interpretations is silly- you, nor I, nor any one person, are not the arbiter of art, or photography, or all things under the sun. Some images will resonate with some users, some won''t. A failure on your behalf to predict the reaction of the masses does not mean your photo is without merit, and some would say this in fact means it deserves more attention for that reason alone, but don''t take this to mean that it is necessarily the audience that is to blame. The onus is on the artist to provide a framework for the viewer.', '2011-08-03 04:29:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(6490313, 963910, 113591, '<td valign="top">Does have that lost to the world, last bit of life look about it.</td>', 'Does have that lost to the world, last bit of life look about it.', '2011-08-03 04:29:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(6490344, 963902, 16106, '<td valign="top">To me this is a fun holiday shot. It looks like it captured a spontaneous moment worth keeping hold of. It does not look like a rigorously thought out, carefully executed and edited work. I do not see a "brilliant idea", and I am glad that the ten people who finished below you did not also rail against the score that their entry received. <br/> <br/>If you like the image and a few others did as well, that ought to be enough. If you want to hear what others think of it, then enter it into competition. However don''t be offended by <i>all</i> their impressions of your work; and railing against a commenter who didn''t like your work, but had the guts to tell you why is not going to help.<br/> <br/>IMHO you didn''t really deserve the ones, nor did you deserve the tens. No one likes ones, and seven of them hurt. I got seven ones on a shot that I thought was spot on the challenge (finished fifth anyway) and no one who hated it had the guts to say what they hated about it. I would have liked to know why. Reject or accept negative reactions as you will, but if you want to score better you need to hear them out.</td>', 'To me this is a fun holiday shot. It looks like it captured a spontaneous moment worth keeping hold of. It does not look like a rigorously thought out, carefully executed and edited work. I do not see a "brilliant idea", and I am glad that the ten people who finished below you did not also rail against the score that their entry received.  If you like the image and a few others did as well, that ought to be enough. If you want to hear what others think of it, then enter it into competition. However don''t be offended by all their impressions of your work; and railing against a commenter who didn''t like your work, but had the guts to tell you why is not going to help. IMHO you didn''t really deserve the ones, nor did you deserve the tens. No one likes ones, and seven of them hurt. I got seven ones on a shot that I thought was spot on the challenge (finished fifth anyway) and no one who hated it had the guts to say what they hated about it. I would have liked to know why. Reject or accept negative reactions as you will, but if you want to score better you need to hear them out.', '2011-08-03 04:54:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(6490375, 963897, 16106, '<td valign="top"><i>Then I thought about putting it in a container. Though when the container fell over, it startled him, an I think he went to the bathroom on the railing... It was all wet, and there was no water in the vase!</i> <br/> <br/>I''m looking up the number for PETA in Va. This abuse of our bushy tailed friends must be put to a stop.<br/> <br/>Beautiful framing and editing on this one, grats. Another (<i>yawn</i>) blue.</td>', 'Then I thought about putting it in a container. Though when the container fell over, it startled him, an I think he went to the bathroom on the railing... It was all wet, and there was no water in the vase!  I''m looking up the number for PETA in Va. This abuse of our bushy tailed friends must be put to a stop. Beautiful framing and editing on this one, grats. Another (yawn) blue.', '2011-08-03 05:40:08.000000', NULL, 0),
